6,Nilo-Saharan languages | African Language Family | Britannica,https://www.britannica.com/topic/Nilo-Saharan-languages,"July 26, 1999 - Most Nilo-Saharan languages are tonal, using pitch to indicate lexical or grammatical differences, though some border on non-tonal languages. Unlike the Afro-Asiatic family, Nilo-Saharan languages generally lack gender distinctions, with only a few exceptions in some Nilotic languages.",Temein,Nilo-Saharan
7,(PDF) Nilo-Saharan: General overview,https://www.researchgate.net/publication/304114105_Nilo-Saharan_General_overview,"January 1, 2016 - Tirmaga-Chai (this volume) with - in Temein with -ga in · Kwama (Siebert and Bryant 2007: 461). The second person singular pronoun -i/-gi in · Kwama may be cognate with Fur ji-, or with the common Nilotic marker for second",Temein,Nilo-Saharan
8,ARGUMENTS FOR THE COHERENCE OF NILO-SAHARAN,https://uonjournals.uonbi.ac.ke/index.php/unjll/article/download/3361/2489/10683,"Nilo-Saharan. Greenberg (1981) calls it a ‘stage III article’ and Ehret (2001: 176, 181) both an “adjective suffix” and a “noun particularizing prefix”. Many languages · across a wide zone of central Africa have these affixes, which have an · underspecified vowel and can be suffixed, prefixed, or infixed. Indeed, sometimes · they are repeated at both ends of the word, as in Temein (Blench 2013).",Temein,Nilo-Saharan
9,Nilo-Saharan Languages,https://languagesgulper.com/eng/Nilo.html,"Overview and Distribution. Nilo-Saharan is one of the four major African language phyla, together with Afro-Asiatic, Niger-Congo and Khoisan.",Temein,Nilo-Saharan
